            {
              "id": "glog-a14c508f-39a9-4d84-a9b7-a29da2288f26",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability associated with the `strlen` function in C++ arises when it is used improperly, particularly with untrusted input. The `strlen` function calculates the length of a string by searching for the null terminator (`'\\0'`). If the input buffer is not null-terminated, `strlen` may read beyond the buffer's bounds, leading to undefined behavior, potential crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate input to ensure it is properly null-terminated before passing it to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions that limit the number of characters read, such as `strnlen`.\n3. **Buffer Management**: Ensure that all buffers are properly initialized and null-terminated.\n4. **Static Analysis**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities in your code.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `strlen(buffer)` with `strnlen(buffer, max_length)` where `max_length` is the maximum number of characters to be read, including the null terminator.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for strnlen\n\nsize_t safe_strlen(const char* buffer, size_t max_length) {\n    return strnlen(buffer, max_length);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const size_t BUFFER_SIZE = 256;\n    char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE] = \"Example string\";\n\n    // Safe usage of strnlen\n    size_t length = safe_strlen(buffer, BUFFER_SIZE);\n    // Proceed with using 'length' safely\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `strnlen` function.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-0efcf5ea-547e-4332-9ee4-8ac877947de0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. The vulnerability arises when the function is used improperly, leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other security issues. In the provided code snippet `strlen(&buf[0])`, the function is used to get the length of a buffer. If the buffer does not contain a null-terminated string, `strlen` will continue reading until it finds a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your buffers are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Avoid using `strlen` on buffers that are not strings. Consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string to be considered.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `buf` is a character array, you should ensure it is null-terminated before using `strlen`. If the size of the buffer is known, consider using `strnlen` instead:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(buf, sizeof(buf));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
